This paper presents Device Intent Blueprint (DIB), a framework designed to facilitate the creation and sharing of MUD policies by separating shared traffic evidence from local authorization. Candidate communications enter a review queue and generate MUD rules only after local approval.  A shared dispute triggers coordinated withdrawal while local decisions are preserved. DIB records the approval validity and the origin of revocation, allowing disconnected sites to reconcile even after missing an entire withdrawal-and-restoration episode.

 We evaluate DIB through {+bounded (finite-instance)+} exhaustive lifecycle model checking, an OpenWrt/osMUD testbed, and experiments on three IoT traffic datasets. The results show that DIB works as intended: it preserves local authorization during coordinated withdrawal and recovery, reconciles [-disconnected sites-]{+a restarted site with median convergence+} within a [-measured bound after reconnection,-]{+pre-registered bound,+} and supports flexible candidate selection. A protocol-level model exposed races between evidence reads and local updates, one of which lets an approval survive a restoration; the corrected design is verified in the model and implemented in the evaluated prototype.[-Overall, the tests confirm that DIB works as intended, with median convergence after reconnection within the pre-registered bound.-] 
\end{abstract}

