\subsection{Roles, Trust Boundary, and Assumptions}
\label{subsec:trust}
Members submit observations, read [-their own candidates,-]{+shared scores,+} and send \emph{dispute notices}, i.e., requests to withdraw a fact. Each site's operator alone approves, revokes, and restores facts at that site. A consortium administrator alone performs global restoration after a dispute. The evidence service aggregates evidence, selects candidates, and orders and distributes lifecycle updates; it is trusted for this, not to authorize. Each site's MUD manager and gateway apply only that site's policy.

The boundary of each site's administrative domain (dashed boxes in Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}) is the only place where authorization is created. Inputs that cross it from outside, such as observations, candidates, disputes, and restorations, can at most place a fact in review or withdraw its rule; they cannot install a rule. The goals rely on six assumptions:
\begin{itemize}
