\section{Implementation and Evaluation Method}
\label{sec:impl}
The distributed prototype separates shared evidence from site-local decisions into distinct services (Sec.~\ref{sec:scalability-results}). [-Registry transactions apply per-fact dispute/restore changes atomically,-]{+Each service is a Python/FastAPI process+} with [-up-]{+its own SQLite database. Opt-in mutual TLS maps the client certificate's SAN URI+} to [-ten retries for conflicting writes. The implementation uses Python-]{+a site+} and [-FastAPI, with SQLAlchemy~2 and SQLite for persistence.-]{+role; without it, endpoints are unauthenticated.+}

[-To test whether lifecycle changes reach gateway-]{+For+} enforcement,[-we run-] unmodified osMUD[-commit-] \texttt{852918e} {+runs+} in a privileged[-Docker container with the-] OpenWrt 23.05.5 [-root filesystem. Restarting osMUD after-]{+container; on+} each[-policy-] change [-applies-]{+the harness exports the site's+} MUD [-ACLs as-]{+file and restarts osMUD, and polls state,+} nftables[-rules. We record ACEs, effective-] rules,[-registry/osMUD state,-] and[-a live-] TCP [-probe for each transition.-]{+connections every 10--50~ms on one clock.+} Exports are unsigned and signature validation is disabled.

Candidate-selection experiments use three traffic datasets. UNSW-IoTraffic~\cite{sivanathan2019classifying} supplies 27 device types and 28 traffic-derived reference profiles; its single capture is shuffled and partitioned round-robin into ten pseudo-sites (seed 42 unless stated). Mon(IoT)r~\cite{moniotr} provides US and UK captures after collapsing VPN variants; YourThings~\cite{alrawi2019yourthings} adds a third deployment for cross-lab comparisons (Sec.~\ref{sec:loo-results}). We retain device-initiated flows with a port and global peer or public name. UNSW associates IPs with same-site HTTP Host and TLS Server Name Indication (SNI) within 24 hours; Mon(IoT)r uses cumulative DNS.

