\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}
We assess whether DIB preserves local authorization during withdrawal and recovery, how fast both are under delay and loss, and what candidate selection achieves on captured traffic. [-Timing experiments-]{+Withdrawal and recovery timing+} run ten site services and an evidence service in Docker on one 16-vCPU KVM node, with \texttt{tc netem} on every container; [-hypotheses-]{+gateway timing runs on a separate shared 16-core host with \texttt{netem} on the evidence service's link only. Hypotheses+} were fixed before the runs. {+Gateway timing predates the target-state guard (Sec.~\ref{subsec:security-analysis}); a smoke run after it matched.+}

\subsection{Authorization and Recovery}
\label{sec:lifecycle-results}
The TLA+ model represents three sites and two facts, distinguishing the dispute opener, confirmer, and bystander. Exhaustive exploration of 4,311,852 states finds no violation of I1--I3 or the export checks: only local commits create fresh authorization, export requires it, and disputes invalidate it without affecting unrelated facts. With one of three sites [-disconnected-]{+disconnected, one fact,+} and local revoke and restore enabled, 48,960 states show no violation. A model of the implemented non-atomic query and commit protocol (two sites, one fact) explores 402,633,492 states with three evidence events and partitions without violations; removing any of its five corrections produces one. Adding failed evidence reads, abandoned reconcile runs, and site crash with restart cleanup, safety holds [-with-]{+in separate configurations:+} three events {+with a failed read and a crash+} (154,363,430 states), {+three sites (712,287,745), and+} partitions,[-two-] lost deliveries, quorum two,[-two facts,-] and [-three sites (712,287,745 states); removing-]{+two facts. Removing+} the cleanup produces a violation.[-Strict suspension at every reachable site holds without failures; with failed reads, a site left behind is repaired by anti-entropy.-] These checks are exhaustive only within their finite configurations; they do not cover gateway timing, concurrent requests per site, three-event convergence with failures, or linearizability.

To identify which safeguards prevent failures, we remove six lifecycle guards in separate model variants, M1--M6. Each produces a distinct authorization failure involving grant handling, site confinement, revocation origin, or export control (Table~\ref{tab:tla}). A separate model of the mTLS authorization rules, extracted from the service code, checks role and site confinement; its ten base configurations find no counterexample and six mutants each violate the property they remove. [-\textbf{[PLACEHOLDER: scope on revocation timing-]{+It assumes mutual TLS+} and [-multi-SAN certificates, to confirm.]}-]{+one identity per certificate.+}

