The registry tests exercise these rules through one- and two-dispute episodes, repeated restoration, and local vetoes recorded before or during withdrawal. Among three restoration policies, only DIB prevents export of a disputed fact without a new commit while preserving every local veto: reusing approvals exports the fact at 12 of 18 non-vetoing sites, and resetting all records to review erases all six vetoes. [-All ten OpenWrt/osMUD-]{+In 210+} gateway [-cases apply the exported policies as expected: MonitorOnly produces no rule, the first-]{+episodes with mutual TLS (100 \emph{lan}, 50 \emph{wan}, 50 \emph{degraded}, 10 with a veto), every+} dispute [-removes the nftables rules,-]{+withdrew its fact+} and [-access resumes only after a global restore-]{+spared an unrelated one: medians of 24~ms to invalidate the site approval (0.42, 1.02~s), 0.38~s to refuse new connections,+} and {+5.49~s to install the policy (5.88, 6.50~s), mostly osMUD's restart, which also paused the unrelated fact for 5.1~s. Established connections were never cut. Restore alone never re-enabled access;+} a fresh [-commit.-]{+commit did; vetoes survived.+}

\subsection{Recovery After [-Disconnection}-]{+a Site Restart}+}
\label{sec:scalability-results}
When a site becomes unreachable, the service retains only the latest pending target for each fact and applies it on reconnection through the same idempotent path, using a monotonic per-fact sequence number; a restore target therefore invalidates an old Active approval even if the preceding dispute never reached the site. We kill one [-site, complete-]{+site while+} $k$ [-withdrawal-and-restore episodes at the others,-]{+distinct endpoints are each disputed, confirmed, and restored,+} restart it, and measure [-the time until it converges,-]{+convergence from its start (a site that stays up while partitioned is not measured),+} for $k=1$, 10, and 50 (50 at \emph{lan} only), reconciliation interval $T\in\{5,15\}$~s, with and without mutual TLS, in three network profiles (\emph{lan}, \emph{wan}: 80~ms and 1\% loss per direction, \emph{degraded}: 200~ms and 5\%). Each of the 28 cells has 30 trials (two series of 10 and 20 trials with different seeds, pooled). The pre-registered bound is $1.5+0.1T+\max(5,D_{run})$~s, 7.0--8.6~s.

The median convergence is 0.6--1.4~s at \emph{lan}, including 50 missed episodes (0.7--1.4~s), 1.4--2.1~s at \emph{wan}, and 2.4--4.1~s at \emph{degraded}; it stays below the bound in all 28 cells. [-All 834 completed-]{+Six of 840+} trials [-(840 planned; six-]{+were+} excluded [-for a-]{+(five+} refused {+setups, one non-empty queue; 16+} setup [-request) end-]{+retries); in the other 834 the restarted site ends+} with every [-site-]{+fact+} in [-the MonitorOnly state and no transient record.-]{+MonitorOnly.+} At \emph{degraded}, 3 of 234 trials exceed the bound (by 0.1 to 4.0~s); in the one we traced, with mutual TLS, the first reconciliation attempt timed out in the TLS handshake (5~s) and the second succeeded.

